00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:19,790
PROFESSOR: But let me
just quickly review

5
00:00:19,790 --> 00:00:22,950
some what we have done
so far in the class.

6
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:32,304
So first, we talked about R
as an ordered set and fields.

7
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:39,805
Then we have talked about
sequences and limits.

8
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:51,523
And then we talked about series.

9
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:58,130
And then, finally, we
have talked about--

10
00:00:58,130 --> 00:01:00,720
and this is where we
will continue today--

11
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:02,960
we talked about metric spaces.

12
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:13,770
And so the idea here
is that we want to--

13
00:01:13,770 --> 00:01:20,760
there's a lot of things from
R that actually generalize

14
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:23,370
and make sense.

15
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:26,500
And some of those are
sequences and limits.

16
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:30,160
So they generalize from
R to metric spaces.

17
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:32,760
There are other things
that don't generalize.

18
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:33,940
It's not an order.

19
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:37,650
A general metric space is
not-- it doesn't necessarily

20
00:01:37,650 --> 00:01:39,550
come with an ordering.

21
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:42,540
And it's not like a field.

22
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:48,190
And in particular, typically,
you can't sum elements.

23
00:01:48,190 --> 00:01:51,490
So a series doesn't
make sense either.

24
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:56,700
But these things here make
sense in a metric space.

25
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:02,540
And so let me just remind
you what is a metric space.

26
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:12,270
So a metric space is a set.

27
00:02:12,270 --> 00:02:14,750
So x is a set.

28
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:17,910
And then there is a
distance function.

29
00:02:17,910 --> 00:02:24,680
So this is a map from
x cross x into r.

30
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:32,240
And for each two pair
of elements x and y,

31
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:35,760
in x you have that--

32
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:40,770
let me make it a
capital X, like this.

33
00:02:40,770 --> 00:02:46,430
For every two elements in this
set, the distance between them

34
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:58,710
should be positive and it should
be nonnegative and equal to 0.

35
00:02:58,710 --> 00:03:00,570
The distance between
two points should

36
00:03:00,570 --> 00:03:06,240
be 0 if and only if these
points are the same.

37
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:10,030
And then it should be symmetric.

38
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:13,540
So property 2 is that
it should be symmetric.

39
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:18,870
So it doesn't matter whether you
go from x to y or from y to x.

40
00:03:18,870 --> 00:03:20,360
The distance is the same.

41
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:28,560
And the third, which is
the most nontrivial part,

42
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:31,180
is the triangle inequality.

43
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:32,676
So this here is symmetric.

44
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:40,080
And the third property
is that if you're

45
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:45,000
looking at x, y, and z,
so you have three points,

46
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:55,720
and you go from x to C, then
it's certainly not further than

47
00:03:55,720 --> 00:04:01,820
if you first go to from x to
y and then go from y to z.

48
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:05,590
So this should be less than or
equal to the distance from x

49
00:04:05,590 --> 00:04:09,760
to y plus the
distance from y to z.

50
00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:14,080
And so this third
property here is what's

51
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:16,214
called the triangle inequality.

52
00:04:19,329 --> 00:04:21,691
So this is the
triangle inequality.

53
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:34,760
Now so that's what
a metric space is.

54
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:44,680
And again, so the canonical
example of a metric space

55
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:49,310
is where you're taking x
equal to the real numbers.

56
00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:52,630
And then for two
true real numbers,

57
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:54,660
the distance between
them is just where

58
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:58,950
you're looking at the
norm of the difference.

59
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:02,010
So this here is a nice distance.

60
00:05:02,010 --> 00:05:03,910
It's clearly nonnegative.

61
00:05:03,910 --> 00:05:08,530
It's clearly equal to 0 if
and only if x is equal to y.

62
00:05:08,530 --> 00:05:10,240
It's clearly symmetric.

63
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:13,020
And the triangle inequality
is this inequality

64
00:05:13,020 --> 00:05:14,655
that we have used all the time.

65
00:05:18,010 --> 00:05:23,790
So this here is slightly more
fancy version of the same

66
00:05:23,790 --> 00:05:28,530
is where you're looking at R2.

67
00:05:28,530 --> 00:05:37,500
So the x here is R2 Or it
could even be a general n.

68
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:44,160
And then if you think
about two elements in Rn

69
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:48,540
and you think about,
say, x here as--

70
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:55,040
it's written as
x1, x2, up to xn,

71
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:58,540
where each of these
xi's are in R,

72
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:02,685
and there are n many of
them, and likewise for y.

73
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:14,830
And then the distance
here the natural distance

74
00:06:14,830 --> 00:06:18,790
is where you're taking--

75
00:06:18,790 --> 00:06:21,290
this is what's called
the Euclidean distance.

76
00:06:21,290 --> 00:06:23,480
It's given by Pythagoras.

77
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:29,420
And so it's the square root
of the sum of the squares,

78
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:39,190
so x1 minus y1 squared plus
x2 minus y2 squared plus

79
00:06:39,190 --> 00:06:46,200
and then all the way up
to xn minus yn squared.

80
00:06:48,890 --> 00:06:53,600
And so you see that if n
here is equal to 1, well,

81
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:59,090
then each element is just
a single element in R.

82
00:06:59,090 --> 00:07:03,170
And so then this thing here
is really the same as that

83
00:07:03,170 --> 00:07:07,580
because it's the
square of x minus y.

84
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:09,413
It's the square
of this distance.

85
00:07:09,413 --> 00:07:10,830
And then you take
the square root.

86
00:07:10,830 --> 00:07:13,220
So it's a norm.

87
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:17,960
So both of these is
called the Euclidean--

88
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:20,470
usually referred to as
a Euclidean distance.

89
00:07:26,990 --> 00:07:32,900
So we looked at a
few other examples.

90
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:38,600
And I won't look at all of
