99
00:08:20,110 --> 00:08:26,590
So this is a plus x2
minus y2, like that.

100
00:08:26,590 --> 00:08:29,210
And this is clearly,
again, symmetric.

101
00:08:29,210 --> 00:08:30,800
It's clearly nonnegative.

102
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:36,679
It's clearly equal to 0 if and
only if x and y are the same.

103
00:08:36,679 --> 00:08:39,280
And the triangle
inequality-- well, that just

104
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:43,360
follows quite easily from
the triangle inequality

105
00:08:43,360 --> 00:08:46,730
for this distance.

106
00:08:46,730 --> 00:08:49,440
Then there is one more example.

107
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:51,800
I mean, there's other--

108
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:54,410
but there's one more
example that is often

109
00:08:54,410 --> 00:08:56,390
useful to keep in mind.

110
00:08:56,390 --> 00:09:01,670
And this is the last
one I will review.

111
00:09:01,670 --> 00:09:05,340
And so this is the
continuous function.

112
00:09:05,340 --> 00:09:14,690
So if you take an interval
a and b are real numbers,

113
00:09:14,690 --> 00:09:16,490
and you're looking
at the interval

114
00:09:16,490 --> 00:09:20,490
from a to b where both
endpoints are included,

115
00:09:20,490 --> 00:09:23,660
and then you look at
the continuous function

116
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:26,660
on this interval.

117
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:32,450
And then if you have
two elements f and g--

118
00:09:32,450 --> 00:09:34,140
so these are continuous.

119
00:09:34,140 --> 00:09:38,330
Each of them are
continuous functions.

120
00:09:38,330 --> 00:09:43,350
Then the distance between
these two continuous functions

121
00:09:43,350 --> 00:09:51,030
is equal to the
max over x And then

122
00:09:51,030 --> 00:09:57,450
f of x minus g of x,
right then, so x here

123
00:09:57,450 --> 00:09:59,711
is in the interval from a to b.

124
00:10:03,990 --> 00:10:06,640
And so this thing here--

125
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:09,780
so this here was on the midterm,
this particular example.

126
00:10:09,780 --> 00:10:14,310
And this thing here
that the max is achieved

127
00:10:14,310 --> 00:10:17,640
is just like if you're looking
at these two functions, then

128
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:20,890
this is the difference
between the two functions.

129
00:10:20,890 --> 00:10:23,170
So this is also continuous.

130
00:10:23,170 --> 00:10:26,400
And this means that the
maximum at the minimum

131
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:28,890
by the extreme value
theorem is achieved

132
00:10:28,890 --> 00:10:30,810
on an interval of this form.

133
00:10:30,810 --> 00:10:34,470
So this thing here
is really achieved,

134
00:10:34,470 --> 00:10:38,050
and it's clearly nonnegative.

135
00:10:38,050 --> 00:10:41,750
And it's easy to see that
it has the other property

136
00:10:41,750 --> 00:10:43,460
of a metric space.

137
00:10:43,460 --> 00:10:47,300
So these are just the
examples that we talked about.

138
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:51,260
And I just want to remind you.

139
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:58,970
And so then the question
is what generalizes from--

140
00:10:58,970 --> 00:11:03,870
so we started with a
class talking about R.

141
00:11:03,870 --> 00:11:07,290
And so it was an order field.

142
00:11:07,290 --> 00:11:08,870
But a lot of the
things we talked

143
00:11:08,870 --> 00:11:11,180
about for R, the
subsequent things

144
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:16,340
we talked about, sequences and
limits, they, as we will see,

145
00:11:16,340 --> 00:11:21,110
make total sense in a
general metric space

146
00:11:21,110 --> 00:11:26,420
and has important
consequences, including

147
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:31,700
to solve differential
equations and solving

148
00:11:31,700 --> 00:11:33,380
all kinds of
equations, including

149
00:11:33,380 --> 00:11:35,900
differential equations.

150
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:44,190
So now let me remind you
also, so we talked about that

151
00:11:44,190 --> 00:11:54,575
if this is a metric space, then
a sequence in a metric space.

152
00:11:59,550 --> 00:12:01,852
This is just a map.

153
00:12:01,852 --> 00:12:10,170
This is a map from the
natural numbers into x.

154
00:12:10,170 --> 00:12:14,190
So a sequence is
like a map like this.

155
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:17,790
And so usually,
the image of the n

156
00:12:17,790 --> 00:12:22,800
element, this we will typically
denote, again, like this, xn.

157
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:25,740
So this is what a sequence is.

158
00:12:25,740 --> 00:12:35,610
A subsequence-- this is
defined very similar to what

159
00:12:35,610 --> 00:12:37,880
we did on the real line.

160
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:41,680
So a subsequence
is the composition

161
00:12:41,680 --> 00:13:06,700
of f with a strictly increasing
map g from the natural numbers

162
00:13:06,700 --> 00:13:08,620
into n.

163
00:13:08,620 --> 00:13:13,600
And then the sequence
is then where

164
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:16,660
you're looking where
you apply first g,

165
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:20,090
and then you apply the f.

166
00:13:20,090 --> 00:13:24,340
So when you first
apply g, then it

167
00:13:24,340 --> 00:13:26,270
gives you another
natural number.

168
00:13:26,270 --> 00:13:28,600
And so this thing
here, the image of this

169
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:30,760
is then a element in x.

170
00:13:30,760 --> 00:13:32,650
So that's a subsequence.

171
00:13:32,650 --> 00:13:35,150
And again, this was
strictly increasing

172
00:13:35,150 --> 00:13:39,750
guarantee you that the elements
appear in the same order.

173
00:13:39,750 --> 00:13:41,580
You're picking a
selection of them,

174
00:13:41,580 --> 00:13:43,160
but they appear
in the same order

175
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:44,770
as in the original sequence.

176
00:13:48,230 --> 00:13:51,670
So sequences and
subsequences make sense.

177
00:13:54,530 --> 00:13:56,667
We can then also talk about--

178
00:13:56,667 --> 00:13:58,250
and I know we talked
about, but I just

179
00:13:58,250 --> 00:14:00,200
want to-- because
of the break, I just

180
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:05,120
want to review quickly a
few of the things we talked

181
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:07,610
about right before the break.

182
00:14:07,610 --> 00:14:22,390
So one is that a convergent
sequence in a metric space--

183
00:14:26,810 --> 00:14:33,640
so if this here is a
metric space and xn

184
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:49,390
is a sequence in xd,
then we say that we

185
00:14:49,390 --> 00:15:02,595
say that xn converges to
x if the following hold.

186
00:15:10,870 --> 00:15:21,310
That for all epsilon
greater than 0,

187
00:15:21,310 --> 00:15:33,320
there exists capital N
such that if little n is

188
00:15:33,320 --> 00:15:40,340
equal to capital N, then
xn the distance between xn

189
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:45,440
and this limit should
be less than epsilon.

190
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:49,790
So that's exact parallel
to how we defined

191
00:15:49,790 --> 00:15:53,660
the limit of a sequence or
that a sequence is converging

192
00:15:53,660 --> 00:15:56,930
if it was a sequence in R.

193
00:15:56,930 --> 00:16:03,320
And likewise, we can define
a sequence in a metric space.

194
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:22,670
So we define a Cauchy sequence
in a metric space x, d.

195
00:16:22,670 --> 00:16:26,460
So this is a sequence
so that for all--

196
00:16:26,460 --> 00:16:35,460
so this is a sequence
xn in such that

197
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:41,040
for all epsilon
greater than 0, there

198
00:16:41,040 --> 00:16:54,930
exists a capital N such that
if little m and little n

199
00:16:54,930 --> 00:16:58,390
are bigger or
equal to capital N.

200
00:16:58,390 --> 00:17:00,210
So remember that
a Cauchy sequence

201
00:17:00,210 --> 00:17:03,190
means that the tail
is bunching together.

202
00:17:03,190 --> 00:17:06,240
So this means that if you
are sufficiently far out,

203
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:13,770
then these elements, xn and
xm, are epsilon close together.

204
00:17:13,770 --> 00:17:17,520
So this is what it
means for a sequence

205
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:20,609
to be a Cauchy sequence.

206
00:17:20,609 --> 00:17:25,050
And so now we have
this theorem, and I

207
00:17:25,050 --> 00:17:27,670
think I mentioned
it before the break.

208
00:17:27,670 --> 00:17:29,780
But let's just
quickly run through it

209
00:17:29,780 --> 00:17:31,940
because it's very short.

210
00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:39,530
So if you have a-- so if
this is a metric space,

211
00:17:39,530 --> 00:17:57,830
then the theorem is that any
convergent sequence is also

212
00:17:57,830 --> 00:17:58,810
a Cauchy sequence.

213
00:18:06,140 --> 00:18:08,005
And the proof is like this.

214
00:18:10,850 --> 00:18:25,680
So given epsilon, since xn
is a convergent sequence,

215
00:18:25,680 --> 00:18:28,680
so you assume that x is a
convergence sequence-- let's

216
00:18:28,680 --> 00:18:30,100
call the limit x.

217
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:35,430
Since xn converges
to x, this means

218
00:18:35,430 --> 00:18:49,590
that there exists capital N
such that if little n is bigger

219
00:18:49,590 --> 00:18:53,610
or equal to capital N, then
the distance between little

220
00:18:53,610 --> 00:19:03,390
x, little n, and nx here, this
distance here is less than--

221
00:19:03,390 --> 00:19:07,200
and then I use epsilon over 2.

222
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:15,570
And so this means
that, therefore,

223
00:19:15,570 --> 00:19:23,220
if little m and little n are
both bigger than capital N then

224
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:26,470
the distance between the two--

225
00:19:26,470 --> 00:19:30,610
distance between these
two elements here,

226
00:19:30,610 --> 00:19:33,790
well, by the
triangle inequality,

227
00:19:33,790 --> 00:19:36,830
we have that this
