248
00:21:37,258 --> 00:21:42,410
Now we looked at a
very simple example.

249
00:21:42,410 --> 00:21:46,790
And if I have time, I will
talk about it a little bit more

250
00:21:46,790 --> 00:21:49,810
interesting example.

251
00:21:49,810 --> 00:21:54,230
Otherwise, I'll talk
about that next time.

252
00:21:54,230 --> 00:22:00,940
So if the metric space was R
with the Euclidean distance,

253
00:22:00,940 --> 00:22:05,570
but then we had a converse-- but
if the metric space was, say--

254
00:22:05,570 --> 00:22:08,510
so we looked at where
the metric space was--

255
00:22:08,510 --> 00:22:13,400
so where x here, the set was
the interval from 0 to 1,

256
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:18,620
and the distance here was just
the usual Euclidean distance.

257
00:22:18,620 --> 00:22:27,290
So then if you're looking at
the sequence xn, 1 over n,

258
00:22:27,290 --> 00:22:32,340
this one here, this is a
sequence in this space.

259
00:22:32,340 --> 00:22:34,640
It's a Cauchy sequence.

260
00:22:34,640 --> 00:22:37,880
But it doesn't
converge in this space

261
00:22:37,880 --> 00:22:42,140
because 0 is not
part of the space.

262
00:22:42,140 --> 00:22:46,490
So this here is a
Cauchy sequence.

263
00:22:46,490 --> 00:22:51,720
So you may say this is a
rather trivial example,

264
00:22:51,720 --> 00:22:54,860
not very interesting.

265
00:22:54,860 --> 00:22:59,510
I'll show you another example
a little bit later or next time

266
00:22:59,510 --> 00:23:04,280
that is more interesting
but, in some sense,

267
00:23:04,280 --> 00:23:07,790
has some of the same feature,
that the space is just not

268
00:23:07,790 --> 00:23:09,450
quite large enough.

269
00:23:09,450 --> 00:23:11,510
But it will seem
more interesting.

270
00:23:11,510 --> 00:23:18,560
So this here is a Cauchy
sequence that is not convergent.

271
00:23:23,590 --> 00:23:32,110
So again, the Cauchy
[INAUDIBLE], so this

272
00:23:32,110 --> 00:23:34,030
leads to the following.

273
00:23:34,030 --> 00:23:36,570
This leads to the
following notion here.

274
00:23:45,520 --> 00:23:47,530
So this is the
following definition

275
00:23:47,530 --> 00:23:52,923
of being Cauchy complete.

276
00:23:57,400 --> 00:24:01,090
So a space or metric
space x comma d

277
00:24:01,090 --> 00:24:22,876
is Cauchy complete if every
Cauchy sequence is convergent.

278
00:24:33,630 --> 00:24:38,700
So now let me define some--

279
00:24:38,700 --> 00:24:44,930
so if we begin with extending
some notions from our--

280
00:24:44,930 --> 00:24:47,600
so the first one
I want to extend

281
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:52,150
is what it means in a metric
space, what is a form?

282
00:24:59,600 --> 00:25:04,040
So we have here--

283
00:25:04,040 --> 00:25:05,650
so a metric form.

284
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:13,590
So we have a metric space.

285
00:25:13,590 --> 00:25:17,710
We have a point in
the metric space.

286
00:25:17,710 --> 00:25:23,970
We have some r which is a real
number, positive real number.

287
00:25:23,970 --> 00:25:29,700
And then we define
a Br of x here.

288
00:25:29,700 --> 00:25:41,610
This here is the set y in x So
that the distance from x to y

289
00:25:41,610 --> 00:25:44,160
is less than or
strictly less than r.

290
00:25:49,830 --> 00:25:53,730
And so this here is called this
here is called the metric ball.

291
00:25:53,730 --> 00:25:57,630
This set here is called
a metric ball or just

292
00:25:57,630 --> 00:26:11,680
a ball with radius r and set x.

293
00:26:18,440 --> 00:26:25,230
Now let me just illustrate it.

294
00:26:25,230 --> 00:26:27,960
So a lot of the intuitions
about metric spaces--

295
00:26:27,960 --> 00:26:31,940
but you have to be careful
because, sometimes,

296
00:26:31,940 --> 00:26:36,980
that intuition fools you just
because metric spaces are

297
00:26:36,980 --> 00:26:38,880
allowed to be very general.

298
00:26:38,880 --> 00:26:41,600
But a lot of the intuition
about metric spaces

299
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:46,560
come from just thinking about
things in Euclidean space.

300
00:26:46,560 --> 00:26:49,790
So when I'm talking
about Euclidean space,

301
00:26:49,790 --> 00:26:53,340
I'm thinking mostly about
it could be on the line.

302
00:26:53,340 --> 00:26:57,920
So in the case where x is
r or it could be in a plane

303
00:26:57,920 --> 00:27:02,730
where x is r2, or it
could be where x is r3.

304
00:27:02,730 --> 00:27:05,490
And so in either of
those three cases,

305
00:27:05,490 --> 00:27:08,810
the distance is the distance
that you're used to,

306
00:27:08,810 --> 00:27:12,630
which is the Euclidean
distance we talked about.

307
00:27:12,630 --> 00:27:18,130
And so in that case, if
you take a point here--

308
00:27:18,130 --> 00:27:21,510
so just thinking
about the example

309
00:27:21,510 --> 00:27:24,210
where you think about
the example where

310
00:27:24,210 --> 00:27:28,360
x is R2, then a ball here--

311
00:27:28,360 --> 00:27:31,920
well, a usual ball is
really just everything

312
00:27:31,920 --> 00:27:35,610
that is the distance r from x.

313
00:27:35,610 --> 00:27:38,410
So this is kind of what
I defined over here.

314
00:27:38,410 --> 00:27:44,440
There's another thing you could
also look at some other sets.

315
00:27:44,440 --> 00:27:52,050
So imagine that you take
a line in the plane,

316
00:27:52,050 --> 00:27:56,850
and you could look at-- or
Maybe this could even be--

317
00:27:56,850 --> 00:27:58,530
it would look more
interesting if I'm

318
00:27:58,530 --> 00:28:02,070
thinking about a line in R3.

319
00:28:02,070 --> 00:28:04,920
So imagine that
this is now-- this

320
00:28:04,920 --> 00:28:07,090
is supposed to look like in R3.

321
00:28:07,090 --> 00:28:08,920
And then you could
look at a line,

322
00:28:08,920 --> 00:28:13,310
and you could look at-- instead
of looking at a metric ball,

323
00:28:13,310 --> 00:28:16,040
you could look at a
tubular neighborhood.

324
00:28:16,040 --> 00:28:17,810
So suppose you're looking at--

325
00:28:17,810 --> 00:28:19,335
that's very similar to a ball.

326
00:28:27,850 --> 00:28:33,080
So a tubular neighborhood, let's
say a r tubular neighborhood--

327
00:28:33,080 --> 00:28:36,700
this is where have
some positive number r

328
00:28:36,700 --> 00:28:43,150
and you're looking at everything
that has distance at most

329
00:28:43,150 --> 00:28:46,970
or let's say strictly less
than r from this line.

330
00:28:46,970 --> 00:28:49,180
So if you do that,
that would look

331
00:28:49,180 --> 00:28:52,600
like a cylinder, where the
boundary of the cylinder

332
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:56,200
is not included because I
require things to have distance

333
00:28:56,200 --> 00:28:59,920
strictly less than r.

334
00:28:59,920 --> 00:29:05,700
This here would be a r tubular
neighborhood of a line.

335
00:29:05,700 --> 00:29:11,050
So this is just to say that
of notion here of a ball,

336
00:29:11,050 --> 00:29:13,690
you could even have
something more general.

337
00:29:13,690 --> 00:29:17,610
And both of these things
that you know well

338
00:29:17,610 --> 00:29:20,430
from Euclidean space,
they make sense

339
00:29:20,430 --> 00:29:22,545
in just a general metric space.

340
00:29:22,545 --> 00:29:25,140
The line maybe
doesn't make sense.

341
00:29:25,140 --> 00:29:28,380
Sometimes, often,
it does, actually.

342
00:29:28,380 --> 00:29:30,700
But that's something
more complicated.

343
00:29:30,700 --> 00:29:33,430
But you could just think
about it as a subset.

344
00:29:33,430 --> 00:29:37,560
And then [? things ?] that have
distance strictly less than r

345
00:29:37,560 --> 00:29:38,355
to that subset.

346
00:29:44,530 --> 00:29:48,324
So this is what it means for--

347
00:29:48,324 --> 00:29:52,620
this is what a ball
is, metric ball.

348
00:29:52,620 --> 00:29:57,930
Then there's the notion of a
subset of a metric space being

349
00:29:57,930 --> 00:30:01,610
bounded, so a bounded subset.

350
00:30:06,400 --> 00:30:09,880
So there's another definition--

351
00:30:09,880 --> 00:30:20,595
bounded subset of
a metric space.

352
00:30:27,820 --> 00:30:31,480
So we have a metric space here.

353
00:30:31,480 --> 00:30:35,890
And we have A as a subset.

354
00:30:35,890 --> 00:30:56,260
And we say that A
is bounded if there

355
00:30:56,260 --> 00:31:02,585
exists x, an element in
this metric space, and d--

356
00:31:05,400 --> 00:31:14,270
d is some positive real
number such that A here

357
00:31:14,270 --> 00:31:21,205
is contained in the ball
of radius d centered at x.

358
00:31:25,976 --> 00:31:30,980
Now with this-- so there's
a few things like this

359
00:31:30,980 --> 00:31:34,770
that we'll see for these things.

360
00:31:34,770 --> 00:31:39,500
The empty set-- if you
take x and you take

361
00:31:39,500 --> 00:31:43,255
the subset that has no
elements, that's the empty set.

362
00:31:45,830 --> 00:31:49,190
The empty set is
contained in everything.

363
00:31:49,190 --> 00:31:52,190
So actually, the empty
set would be bounded.

364
00:31:52,190 --> 00:31:57,920
But it's not a particularly
interesting example

365
00:31:57,920 --> 00:31:58,910
of a bounded set.

366
00:31:58,910 --> 00:32:03,340
And it may be that the whole
metric space is possible

367
00:32:03,340 --> 00:32:06,010
that the whole metric
space is bounded

368
00:32:06,010 --> 00:32:09,110
if it was like an interval.

369
00:32:09,110 --> 00:32:12,230
If it was just an
interval where both--

370
00:32:12,230 --> 00:32:14,500
so suppose you have
some interval from A

371
00:32:14,500 --> 00:32:18,580
to B. Then this thing here is--

372
00:32:18,580 --> 00:32:22,480
if you think about the usual
distance on that interval,

373
00:32:22,480 --> 00:32:25,120
then that's a bounded set.

374
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:27,010
The whole set x is bounded.

375
00:32:27,010 --> 00:32:32,230
So in particular, any subset
would also be bounded.

376
00:32:32,230 --> 00:32:40,300
So now let me just prove
one theorem, one lemma, that

377
00:32:40,300 --> 00:32:44,930
is another thing that
generalizes from R.

378
00:32:44,930 --> 00:32:48,230
And that is that if you
take a Cauchy sequence--

379
00:32:48,230 --> 00:32:51,730
so any Cauchy
sequence is bounded.

380
00:32:51,730 --> 00:32:53,265
So let's prove this.

381
00:32:57,770 --> 00:33:05,750
So we have, again,
a metric space.

382
00:33:05,750 --> 00:33:08,755
xn is a Cauchy sequence.

383
00:33:16,130 --> 00:33:23,990
And then we think about
the set xn minus--

384
00:33:23,990 --> 00:33:27,870
so this is this subset of x.

385
00:33:27,870 --> 00:33:32,940
And I claim that this subset
here, this subset here--

386
00:33:32,940 --> 00:33:35,965
so A here is bounded.

387
00:33:40,760 --> 00:33:43,923
Now why is that the case?

388
00:33:49,760 --> 00:34:04,980
So since xn is a sequence,
if I choose epsilon with--

389
00:34:04,980 --> 00:34:10,770
so with epsilon,
say, equal to 1,

390
00:34:10,770 --> 00:34:22,920
you have that there exists
capital N so that if little n--

391
00:34:22,920 --> 00:34:29,520
such that if little
n and little m

392
00:34:29,520 --> 00:34:34,050
bigger than capital N, then
the distance between the two

393
00:34:34,050 --> 00:34:39,760
is smaller than 1.

394
00:34:39,760 --> 00:34:42,210
This is just using that
as a Cauchy sequence

395
00:34:42,210 --> 00:34:48,179
and just using one particular
epsilon, namely taking epsilon

396
00:34:48,179 --> 00:34:51,360
to be equal to 1.

397
00:34:51,360 --> 00:34:53,850
So now, of course, this means--

398
00:34:53,850 --> 00:34:57,720
and now you don't need
this event for all of this.

399
00:34:57,720 --> 00:35:00,180
You don't need it like this.

400
00:35:00,180 --> 00:35:06,830
So in particular,
therefore, as long

401
00:35:06,830 --> 00:35:11,210
as n is bigger than capital
N, well, you can use one--

402
00:35:11,210 --> 00:35:13,550
or you can use m here.

403
00:35:13,550 --> 00:35:17,130
You can use capital N for m.

404
00:35:17,130 --> 00:35:19,160
So then this here--
say, therefore,

405
00:35:19,160 --> 00:35:26,690
for little n bigger than this,
we have that this thing here,

406
00:35:26,690 --> 00:35:29,570
the distance to this
is smaller than 1.

407
00:35:33,620 --> 00:35:41,870
And so now you have
that if you now

408
00:35:41,870 --> 00:35:52,380
set r equal to 1 plus the
maximum of the distance

409
00:35:52,380 --> 00:36:07,110
from xN, x capital N,
to xi, where i here runs

410
00:36:07,110 --> 00:36:11,410
from 1 of 2 capital N minus 1.

411
00:36:11,410 --> 00:36:17,310
So there are n minus 1 of these
that you take the maximum over.

412
00:36:17,310 --> 00:36:22,710
So if you define little r to
be like this, then obviously,

413
00:36:22,710 --> 00:36:24,700
little r is some
positive number.

414
00:36:24,700 --> 00:36:25,830
It's finite.

415
00:36:31,620 --> 00:36:37,170
And now I claim that our set A
here is contained in this ball

416
00:36:37,170 --> 00:36:39,850
here around xn.

417
00:36:39,850 --> 00:36:42,900
So I claim this thing here.

418
00:36:57,190 --> 00:36:58,070
Now let's see.

419
00:36:58,070 --> 00:37:00,580
So why is this the case?

420
00:37:00,580 --> 00:37:06,050
So if x here--

421
00:37:06,050 --> 00:37:08,830
so we need to prove that--

422
00:37:08,830 --> 00:37:24,070
we need to show that
for all n you have

423
00:37:24,070 --> 00:37:31,840
that the distance
here is less than r.

424
00:37:31,840 --> 00:37:34,870
If that's the case,
then the whole set

425
00:37:34,870 --> 00:37:39,310
here would lie in this ball.

426
00:37:39,310 --> 00:37:44,140
But you see that if n here--

427
00:37:44,140 --> 00:37:50,470
well, if n is bigger or equal
to capital N, but this--

428
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:03,250
so we need to show this in here.

429
00:38:03,250 --> 00:38:06,180
If little n is bigger
than capital N,

430
00:38:06,180 --> 00:38:11,730
then we have that the distance
from this little n to this one

431
00:38:11,730 --> 00:38:13,960
here by the tail--

432
00:38:13,960 --> 00:38:16,320
the tail was less than 1.

433
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:21,510
But this thing here is actually
why-- this is actually--

434
00:38:21,510 --> 00:38:25,930
this is equal to r because
it was 1 plus this maximum.

435
00:38:25,930 --> 00:38:29,170
And then this is maximum
over nonnegative numbers.

436
00:38:29,170 --> 00:38:36,720
So you see that for the whole
tail, we have this property.

437
00:38:36,720 --> 00:38:43,150
But for all the other
ones, for n less than

438
00:38:43,150 --> 00:38:50,440
or equal less than n minus 1,
these are the remaining ones.

439
00:38:50,440 --> 00:39:00,530
There, the distance from xn
to x capital N, where it's--

440
00:39:00,530 --> 00:39:02,810
this is one of these guys.

441
00:39:02,810 --> 00:39:04,870
So it's clearly this one here.

442
00:39:04,870 --> 00:39:09,800
It's clearly less
than capital R.

443
00:39:09,800 --> 00:39:11,800
And it's really
strictly less than

444
00:39:11,800 --> 00:39:13,460
because there's this one also.

445
00:39:13,460 --> 00:39:14,650
Yeah?

446
00:39:14,650 --> 00:39:17,330
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] does
it has to be 1 plus the max,

447
00:39:17,330 --> 00:39:20,650
or could it be the max of
1 or all of those elements

448
00:39:20,650 --> 00:39:22,810
of the sequence?

449
00:39:22,810 --> 00:39:26,410
PROFESSOR: I wanted this
one here to be strict.

450
00:39:26,410 --> 00:39:28,040
It's just a silly thing.

451
00:39:28,040 --> 00:39:30,670
But that's why I
took it like that.

452
00:39:30,670 --> 00:39:32,450
Because otherwise
this would be strict.

453
00:39:32,450 --> 00:39:38,190
But it's just a trivial thing,
and it doesn't matter very much.

454
00:39:38,190 --> 00:39:40,190
At this stage, it makes
no difference, actually.

455
00:39:40,190 --> 00:39:43,120
But it was just to use this.

456
00:39:43,120 --> 00:39:45,020
We'll talk a little bit
more about the balls

457
00:39:45,020 --> 00:39:50,420
because there is another way
you could define the ball.

458
00:39:50,420 --> 00:39:52,480
Maybe I'll say a few
words about that now.

459
00:39:55,500 --> 00:40:00,680
So what this proves, again,
is that in any metric space,

460
00:40:00,680 --> 00:40:05,810
a Cauchy sequence is
automatically bounded.

461
00:40:05,810 --> 00:40:07,640
And so remember, in
any metric space,

462
00:40:07,640 --> 00:40:10,080
a convergent sequence
is a Cauchy sequence.

463
00:40:10,080 --> 00:40:13,610
So a convergent sequence
is also bounded.

464
00:40:13,610 --> 00:40:18,060
And now there's this question,
which is a natural question,

465
00:40:18,060 --> 00:40:22,550
and this is connected to
the definition of ball.

466
00:40:22,550 --> 00:40:26,030
So the way we define-- and
it's actually worthwhile

467
00:40:26,030 --> 00:40:31,550
emphasizing because it's
useful in the next thing

468
00:40:31,550 --> 00:40:34,280
that we want to get to.

469
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:39,860
And so when I define
a ball, I assumed--

470
00:40:39,860 --> 00:40:45,360
so the definition of the ball--
just write it up again here.

471
00:40:45,360 --> 00:40:48,780
So the definition of a
ball in a metric space--

472
00:40:48,780 --> 00:40:56,550
these are the y in this set so
that the distance to the center

473
00:40:56,550 --> 00:40:59,670
is strictly less than r.

474
00:40:59,670 --> 00:41:05,070
You could, of course, have
defined it differently.

475
00:41:05,070 --> 00:41:08,550
You could define it
that this is strictly

476
00:41:08,550 --> 00:41:11,740
less or is there strictly
less than or equal to--

477
00:41:11,740 --> 00:41:15,540
I want to use
strictly less here.

478
00:41:15,540 --> 00:41:19,300
But sometimes, if I
want to be very clear,

479
00:41:19,300 --> 00:41:21,970
I will talk about
open and closed ball.

480
00:41:21,970 --> 00:41:24,010
And this is what
we're getting to next.

481
00:41:24,010 --> 00:41:26,470
And so when I'm talking
about open ball--

482
00:41:26,470 --> 00:41:29,760
but I'll try not to
use that term here.

483
00:41:29,760 --> 00:41:33,060
But open would always mean this.

484
00:41:33,060 --> 00:41:37,570
Closed ball would mean that
it's less than or equal to.

485
00:41:37,570 --> 00:41:39,210
Sorry, yeah?

486
00:41:39,210 --> 00:41:43,370
AUDIENCE: Would you talk to me
about the neighborhood example?

487
00:41:43,370 --> 00:41:46,940
Is that reverse-- is x
in that case a subset?

488
00:41:46,940 --> 00:41:48,860
PROFESSOR: Yeah,
that's right, exactly.

489
00:41:48,860 --> 00:41:49,710
That's right.

490
00:41:52,640 --> 00:41:57,110
And I didn't actually--

491
00:41:57,110 --> 00:41:59,400
I already posted
the lecture notes.

492
00:41:59,400 --> 00:42:01,760
And so in the lecture
notes, I didn't actually

493
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:03,360
talk about tubular
neighborhoods.

494
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:09,210
But it maybe is
worth thinking about.

495
00:42:09,210 --> 00:42:13,160
So you could look at it as--

496
00:42:13,160 --> 00:42:15,205
again, you have a
metric space here.

497
00:42:18,440 --> 00:42:21,095
And you could have a subset--

498
00:42:21,095 --> 00:42:25,850
A has a subset, any subset,
of the metric space.

499
00:42:25,850 --> 00:42:29,490
And you could define the tubular
neighborhood of a subset.

500
00:42:29,490 --> 00:42:32,460
And so you could
do this similarly.

501
00:42:32,460 --> 00:42:37,340
So this would be the y
in x so that the distance

502
00:42:37,340 --> 00:42:49,980
from y to x here is
strictly less than r

503
00:42:49,980 --> 00:42:59,830
for some x in a [INAUDIBLE].

504
00:42:59,830 --> 00:43:02,800
So this is how--

505
00:43:02,800 --> 00:43:07,020
so again, if you think about
our tree, you take some--

506
00:43:07,020 --> 00:43:08,940
I mean, maybe we
shouldn't talk about--

507
00:43:08,940 --> 00:43:13,440
maybe we should draw it more
crazy, like some curve, maybe.

508
00:43:13,440 --> 00:43:18,040
And if you're looking at the
set that have distance at most--

509
00:43:18,040 --> 00:43:20,958
this is some sort
of tube, like this.

510
00:43:24,660 --> 00:43:27,220
This here would be
the [INAUDIBLE].

511
00:43:27,220 --> 00:43:30,720
And then here, at the end,
it would look like a cap,

512
00:43:30,720 --> 00:43:33,150
like this.

513
00:43:33,150 --> 00:43:35,430
This here would be like
a tubular neighborhood

514
00:43:35,430 --> 00:43:36,520
of this curve.

515
00:43:39,280 --> 00:43:44,950
And so, as you're
saying that in the case

516
00:43:44,950 --> 00:43:49,730
where A here is just a point,
then those two coincide.

517
00:43:53,060 --> 00:43:57,370
So now one of the
key things when

518
00:43:57,370 --> 00:44:00,760
you're thinking about
convergence in R,

519
00:44:00,760 --> 00:44:04,810
remember that this stuff, when
we use this time and time again,

520
00:44:04,810 --> 00:44:06,220
that it was often--

521
00:44:06,220 --> 00:44:09,940
we saw this for series,
that it was often

522
00:44:09,940 --> 00:44:15,200
difficult to determine the
exact limit of a series.

523
00:44:15,200 --> 00:44:19,550
But the question was
always, does it converge?

524
00:44:19,550 --> 00:44:26,410
And so if it converged, if
it converged, then often, I

525
00:44:26,410 --> 00:44:29,320
mean, also, if you would
have using this in some

526
00:44:29,320 --> 00:44:34,210
of application, it's often
in all kind of fields

527
00:44:34,210 --> 00:44:37,890
that it comes up that things are
written in terms of a series.

528
00:44:37,890 --> 00:44:41,850
And it's not clear what
it's converting to.

529
00:44:41,850 --> 00:44:43,920
But you want to know
whether it's converting.

530
00:44:43,920 --> 00:44:45,990
And then you also
want to-- so you

531
00:44:45,990 --> 00:44:50,460
want to estimate what is the
limit within a certain error

532
00:44:50,460 --> 00:44:51,070
term.

533
00:44:51,070 --> 00:44:53,640
And you can use some of
these comparison tests

534
00:44:53,640 --> 00:44:59,700
to determine-- to
estimate how close you

535
00:44:59,700 --> 00:45:01,510
are to the actual limit.

536
00:45:01,510 --> 00:45:07,260
But in any case, the
thing is it's very useful

537
00:45:07,260 --> 00:45:11,820
to have a criteria that
is easy to work with

538
00:45:11,820 --> 00:45:16,170
and that say that it's
converging without having

539
00:45:16,170 --> 00:45:18,100
to be able to find the limits.

540
00:45:18,100 --> 00:45:21,610
And like the Cauchy sequence,
this was like a notion of that.

541
00:45:21,610 --> 00:45:30,450
And so again, the key on
R, the key for using that

542
00:45:30,450 --> 00:45:34,510
was the Bolzano-Weierstrass
theorem.

543
00:45:34,510 --> 00:45:39,190
So this was the one that
was the key for using to get

544
00:45:39,190 --> 00:45:41,690
the quasi convergence theorem.

545
00:45:41,690 --> 00:45:43,620
So the Bolzano-Weierstrass
theorem--

546
00:45:52,660 --> 00:45:58,570
so remember that this
is for any [INAUDIBLE]

547
00:45:58,570 --> 00:46:11,240
that any bounded sequence
has a convergent subsequence.

548
00:46:22,390 --> 00:46:25,270
That was
Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem.

549
00:46:25,270 --> 00:46:29,440
In a general metric
space, it makes sense

550
00:46:29,440 --> 00:46:35,510
to talk about a
bounded sequence.

551
00:46:35,510 --> 00:46:39,020
But it's not always the
case that a bounded sequence

552
00:46:39,020 --> 00:46:40,830
has a convergent subsequence.

553
00:46:40,830 --> 00:46:42,570
So it does not hold.

554
00:46:42,570 --> 00:46:44,070
The Bolzano does not hold.

555
00:46:44,070 --> 00:46:51,920
So warning,
Bolzano-Weierstrass does not

556
00:46:51,920 --> 00:46:59,200
hold in a general metric space.

557
00:47:08,600 --> 00:47:15,050
And in fact, of course, the
example that we looked at,

558
00:47:15,050 --> 00:47:18,000
where the example was
x here, like this,

559
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:20,010
and when we're looking
at that sequence,

560
00:47:20,010 --> 00:47:22,820
this is a bounded sequence.

561
00:47:22,820 --> 00:47:27,150
But it's bounded, but it doesn't
have a convergent subsequence.

562
00:47:27,150 --> 00:47:28,980
It's actually a Cauchy sequence.

563
00:47:28,980 --> 00:47:30,110
But it doesn't [INAUDIBLE].

564
00:47:30,110 --> 00:47:34,360
So it doesn't hold in
a general metric space,

565
00:47:34,360 --> 00:47:51,960
but it does hold if the
metric space is compact.

566
00:47:54,850 --> 00:47:57,320
And so that's what I
want to talk about next.

567
00:47:57,320 --> 00:48:01,480
But in order to talk about
what it means for metric space

568
00:48:01,480 --> 00:48:09,610
to be compact, then I
need to talk about--

569
00:48:09,610 --> 00:48:15,700
I need to define what it
means for a set to be open.

570
00:48:15,700 --> 00:48:36,500
So we need the notion of
an open set, an open subset

571
00:48:36,500 --> 00:48:42,340
of a metric space.

572
00:48:49,520 --> 00:48:54,530
So we have a metric space again.

573
00:48:54,530 --> 00:49:00,050
And so A here is said to be--

574
00:49:00,050 --> 00:49:02,610
let's call it O for open.

575
00:49:02,610 --> 00:49:21,470
O is said to be an open
subset if for all x in O--

576
00:49:21,470 --> 00:49:28,440
so the idea is that you
have here, this here is O.

577
00:49:28,440 --> 00:49:34,410
So this is maybe O.

578
00:49:34,410 --> 00:49:37,770
And so the idea is
that for each element,

579
00:49:37,770 --> 00:49:41,920
there exists some ball
centered at that point.

580
00:49:41,920 --> 00:49:47,090
So for each x for all
x in O, there exists.

581
00:49:52,080 --> 00:49:55,410
r greater than 0-- it
may be incredibly small--

582
00:49:55,410 --> 00:50:02,310
so that not just x but
the whole ball around x

583
00:50:02,310 --> 00:50:04,180
is contained in the open set.

584
00:50:04,180 --> 00:50:07,710
So you see that not
just this point here,

585
00:50:07,710 --> 00:50:12,510
but the whole ball here would
have to be contained in O.

586
00:50:12,510 --> 00:50:18,040
So in this picture here,
when I'm illustrating this,

587
00:50:18,040 --> 00:50:22,330
I'm thinking about
x as being R2.

588
00:50:22,330 --> 00:50:24,210
And I'm thinking
about the distance

589
00:50:24,210 --> 00:50:25,870
being the usual
Euclidean distance.

590
00:50:28,640 --> 00:50:31,080
But then, actually,
if this is open,

591
00:50:31,080 --> 00:50:35,660
it means that you cannot include
these points here on this curve.

592
00:50:35,660 --> 00:50:38,220
They would not be
included in the set

593
00:50:38,220 --> 00:50:42,960
because if you take any point on
